5:[["$","$L10","1.1",{"topic":{"id":"1.1","title":"Introduction to Law and Cyberspace","minutes":10,"summary":"The internet changes the scale of human activity, not the need for lawful authority. Begin with the people, systems, and legal interests behind every digital interaction.","sections":[{"title":"What cyberspace actually contains","body":["Cyberspace is the interconnected environment in which people create, exchange, and process digital information. It includes physical infrastructure such as cables, routers, data centres, and phones; logical layers such as addresses, protocols, accounts, and applications; and a human layer of identities, organizations, and decisions. A single message may pass through a handset, a local network, a service provider, and an overseas cloud service.","The internet is a network of networks, whereas the World Wide Web is one service carried over it. Email, messaging, online games, and payment APIs are not simply web pages. This distinction matters when identifying evidence: a browser history will not necessarily record a background application’s network connections. “Online” does not mean that the underlying device or server is physically located in the same country as its user."]},{"title":"Why law applies across borders","body":["Cyber law is not one isolated code. Contract law can govern an online purchase, privacy principles can constrain the handling of personal data, criminal law can punish dishonest conduct, and procedural law governs investigation and trial. The Information Technology Act supplies technology-specific provisions, while constitutional protections and judicial decisions shape how those powers operate.","Jurisdiction concerns the authority to prescribe, adjudicate, and enforce rules. A victim in India, an accused elsewhere, and a platform holding records in a third country create different questions. Sections 1 and 75 of the IT Act address specified extraterritorial situations; they do not authorize Indian officers to search a foreign server unilaterally. Mutual legal assistance, letters rogatory, treaty mechanisms, and provider requests must be distinguished."]},{"title":"A rights-based investigation","body":["An investigator protects both the integrity of evidence and the rights of the people connected to it. Authority, necessity, proportionality, purpose limitation, confidentiality, and accountability are practical safeguards. A complaint about one account does not automatically justify downloading every family photograph from a household device.","Start an investigation file with a precise question, its legal basis, the systems and date range in scope, and the least intrusive collection method. Record uncertainty instead of converting suspicion into fact. For urgent financial fraud, preserve transaction references and contact the bank and official reporting channels promptly; classroom technical experiments must never delay victim assistance."]}],"practice":{"title":"Map a single message","scenario":"Asha in Pune sends a payment instruction from her phone to a supplier whose mailbox is hosted abroad.","steps":["List the human, physical, and logical components in the message’s journey.","Identify three possible evidence sources: the handset, the mail service, and the recipient’s device.","Separate the question “Who controlled the account?” from “Where was the server?”.","Write one lawful local collection step and one question requiring provider or cross-border assistance."],"debrief":"A useful map records custody and control as well as location. The existence of an overseas server does not prevent preservation of the original message on Asha’s lawfully accessed phone."},"terms":[],"lawIds":["it-act","bnss"],"sources":["itact","constitution","cybercrime"],"labId":"$undefined","unit":1,"objectives":["Explain what cyberspace actually contains and apply it to an investigation.","Explain why law applies across borders and apply it to an investigation.","Explain a rights-based investigation and apply it to an investigation."],"question":{"id":"q-1.1","prompt":"Which statement best distinguishes the internet from the Web?","options":["They are different names for exactly the same service.","The Web is one application supported by the broader internet.","The internet contains no physical infrastructure.","Only the Web can carry digital evidence."],"answer":1,"explanation":"The internet supports many services, including email and messaging; the Web is one of them.","unit":1}}}]]
